  "report": "<precondition>\nThe attacker needs only an authenticated (any) EatHub account \u2014 no relationship to the victim, no special seeded state. `POST /api/recipes` is gated solely by `isLoggedIn` (routes/recipe.js:58, google_strategy.js:38\u201143: `if (request.user) return done()`), which just checks a valid Passport session; it does not check ownership of anything or any relationship to another user. Account creation is fully self-service: `POST /api/signup` (routes/user.js:32) plus `GET /verify-email` need only an email address the attacker controls \u2014 in a real deployment the verification link is emailed to the address the attacker supplied (local_strategy.js:43\u201148, mailer.js), so the attacker verifies their own account with no interaction from anyone else. (The dev fallback that prints the link to the server console, mailer.js:33\u201137, is irrelevant to real-world exploitability \u2014 it only fires because SMTP is unconfigured in this sandbox.)\n\nThe upload-type check is the actual missing control: `file_uploud.js:17\u201119` \u2014 `fileFilter: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, file.mimetype.startsWith('image/'))` \u2014 trusts the client-supplied `Content-Type`/mimetype and accepts `image/svg+xml` unconditionally. The stored filename keeps the client-supplied extension (`file_uploud.js:10`: `req.recipeId + path.extname(file.originalname).toLowerCase()`), so `evil.svg` is written to disk as `<recipe_id>.svg`. The serving route, `GET /data/recipes_pics/:filename` (routes/recipe.js:279\u2011286), has **no auth middleware at all** and does `res.sendFile()` with no `Content-Type` override, no `Content-Disposition`, and the app sets no `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` or CSP anywhere (grep across index.js and all routes found none). I confirmed the serving route needs zero session state by fetching it from a session (`mallory_anon`) that never logged in \u2014 200 with the raw payload body. So the bar is: attacker registers one account (trivial, self-serve), uploads one recipe with an SVG \"image\", done.\n</precondition>\n\n<capability>\nThis is a stored XSS via unrestricted-file-type upload, not merely a content-type label mismatch. The attacker's SVG (containing `<script>...</script>`) is written verbatim to `./data/recipes_pics/<recipe_id>.svg` and served back with `Content-Type: image/svg+xml` and no protective headers. Any browser that loads that URL as a document (direct navigation, `target=_blank`, `<iframe>`, `<object data=\u2026>`) executes the attacker's JavaScript in the EatHub origin, with the victim's session attached.\n\nI verified the practical reach of that primitive with a second, independently authenticated victim session (`bob`): after `bob` logs in and simply views the poisoned recipe via the ordinary public API (`GET /api/recipes/${rid}` \u2192 `\"photo\":\"/data/recipes_pics/<id>.svg\"`), fetching that photo URL returns the executable payload verbatim under `bob`'s session \u2014 i.e. bob's browser, if it renders that URL as a document, executes attacker JS with bob's own cookies present. The session cookie itself is `httpOnly` (session_config.js:25), so raw cookie theft via `document.cookie` is blocked, but that is largely moot: script running same-origin can call any `/api/*` endpoint with the victim's credentials attached automatically (default `fetch()` same-origin credentials) \u2014 read `/api/profile`, read/enumerate `/api/recipes/mine`, create spam/malicious recipes as the victim (`POST /api/recipes`, propagating the worm), delete the victim's recipes (`DELETE /api/recipes/:id`), post comments/likes as the victim, or log the victim out (`POST /api/logout`). No CSP is configured, so the script can also exfiltrate this data to an attacker-controlled origin over the open internet in a real deployment (the sandbox's lack of egress is an artifact of this test environment, not a mitigation). There is no password/email-change endpoint in the app (checked routes/user.js, routes/recipe.js), so this doesn't grant a direct path to permanently steal the credential itself \u2014 but it grants full \"act as the victim\" capability across the app's authenticated surface for as long as the victim's session is open.\n</capability>\n\n<reachability>\nREACHABLE \u2014 `POST /api/recipes` (multipart, `isLoggedIn`) to store the payload, then `GET /data/recipes_pics/<recipe_id>.svg` (no auth, no session, no headers required) to serve it. Confirmed directly: a session that never authenticated (`mallory_anon`) got HTTP 200 with the raw `<script>` body from that route. The malicious recipe is also automatically discoverable through completely ordinary, unauthenticated app usage \u2014 `GET /api/recipes` (recipe.js:287, public \"most liked\" listing) and `GET /api/recipes/:id` (recipe.js:306, public, no `isLoggedIn`) both return the `photo` field pointing straight at the attacker's SVG, so the poisoned link surfaces in normal browsing, not just via a crafted URL the attacker has to distribute out-of-band. None of the unconfigured integrations (Google OAuth, SMTP, Gemini) are on this path \u2014 it only touches local auth + recipe CRUD + the static image route, exactly the surface stated as reachable for this target, and it doesn't depend on the seeded fixture beyond \"some authenticated user exists,\" which any attacker can create for themselves.\n\nOne caveat: the frontend source (`/work/frontend`) is not present in this checkout (only a built `dist/index.html` stub), so I can't cite the exact markup that turns this URL into a script-executing context in the shipped UI (e.g., whether the photo is rendered via `<img>`, which most modern browsers sandbox from executing inline SVG script, versus a \"view full photo\" link that opens the URL directly, which would execute it). That does not affect reachability of the vulnerable route itself \u2014 an attacker can hand the raw `/data/recipes_pics/<id>.svg` URL to a victim through any channel (chat, forum, the recipe's own public page) independent of how the frontend happens to embed it \u2014 but it does leave open whether the stock UI creates a *zero-extra-effort* trigger path. I mark the underlying primitive REACHABLE and the \"triggered purely by organic in-app browsing with no extra link-sharing\" sub-claim UNCLEAR.\n</reachability>\n\n<blast_radius>\nUnbounded on the victim side, one attacker upload is enough. There is no per-viewer restriction on the serving route (verified: an anonymous, never-authenticated session and a normal authenticated victim session both got the identical 200 + payload response from the same stored file), so every user of the deployment \u2014 anonymous visitors and every registered account \u2014 who opens that one URL is a potential victim from a single attacker action. Each visit compromises that specific victim's own session/account (their recipes, comments, likes, profile read, forced logout) \u2014 the damage is per-victim rather than one shared global counter, but the number of victims is unbounded and grows with exposure (the recipe is also surfaced automatically through the public `/api/recipes` listing, so exposure isn't attacker-limited). Nothing in the multer/storage layer limits an attacker to one payload \u2014 they can upload as many `<recipe_id>.svg` files as they can create recipes (5MB size cap, `file_uploud.js:16`, is the only constraint), seeding an arbitrary number of stored triggers across the `recipes` table and `data/recipes_pics/` directory.\n</blast_radius>\n\n<persistence>\nDurable. `file_uploud.js`'s `multer.diskStorage` writes the SVG to `./data/recipes_pics/` on the real filesystem (not memory/tmpfs), and the corresponding `recipes.photo` DB row points at it \u2014 both survive an application/process restart, since they are ordinary persisted application state, the same way any uploaded recipe photo would be. There is no scanning, sanitization, or re-check of stored files after upload. Cleanup exists but is owner-triggered only: `DELETE /api/recipes/:id` (recipe.js:86\u2011114) unlinks the photo file when the *recipe owner* deletes their own recipe (`fs.unlink`, recipe.js:104), and even that is best-effort (\"a leftover file is not worth failing the request over\"). So the payload stays live and servable indefinitely until the attacker who planted it (or someone with DB/filesystem access) removes it \u2014 there's no admin moderation route in scope, and no automatic expiry.\n</persistence>\n\n<escalation_attempt>\nNot executed (no real JS engine available in the harness to prove script execution end-to-end), but two chains are worth flagging as feasibility notes:\n1. **CORS overlap.** `index.js:43\u201144` configures `cors({ origin: clientUrl || true, credentials: true })`; when `CLIENT_URL` is unset (as in this build) this reflects *any* request origin and allows credentialed requests. If that also holds in a real deployment, it independently widens the impact of any XSS reached via this bug \u2014 exfiltration doesn't need same-origin `fetch()` tricks, an attacker page can be issued credentialed cross-origin requests directly. This is a separate, adjacent misconfiguration and not verified in depth here, but it compounds the severity of the SVG-XSS primitive if both ship together.\n2. **Worm propagation.** Since the XSS runs with the victim's session and `POST /api/recipes` is reachable, injected script could re-upload another malicious SVG \"as\" the victim, turning a single seed recipe into a self-propagating stored-XSS chain across accounts that view any infected recipe \u2014 I did not implement this, but the API surface supports it (no CSRF token requirement observed on `POST /api/recipes`, only session cookie, and `sameSite: 'lax'` still permits top-level navigations/most same-site XHR).\n</escalation_attempt>\n\n<novelty>NOT_CHECKED</novelty>\n\n<severity>HIGH \u2014 Any self-registered attacker (trivial precondition, no relationship to victim needed) can persistently plant script-executing SVG content behind a completely unauthenticated, header-unprotected static route that is automatically surfaced through normal, public recipe browsing; a victim who opens that URL gets attacker JS executed in the app's origin with their session attached, granting the attacker the victim's full in-app capability set (read profile, create/delete recipes, comment/like, log out) until the payload is removed. It falls short of CRITICAL because it requires a victim to load the URL as a document (not proven zero-click from the stock UI without frontend source), httpOnly cookies aren't directly exfiltrable, and there is no in-app path from this alone to a permanent credential/account takeover (no password/email-change endpoint exists to abuse).</severity>"
